{
  "focus": "defects in the change. Check, in this order:
1. Correctness: wrong results, missed edge cases, null or undefined handling, races, broken invariants.
2. Security: injection, authentication or permission bypass, secret or token exposure, unsafe handling of untrusted input, resource exhaustion or denial of service from outside input (CWE-400, CWE-770), such as an allocation, a loop or recursion sized by the input, or a size limit removed or raised. For a library, outside input is anything a caller of its public API can pass, readers and byte streams included. A security defect always blocks the merge, so report every one you find.
3. Reference claims: when the PR title, description or changed code says the change matches another implementation, a spec or a standard, open the code or text it names and compare it with the change line by line. Read it in a **reference** related repo when one is listed. Report each difference the PR does not call out, citing both sides.
4. Reference behaviour: when the code implements or mirrors a reference, such as a spec, another implementation or an API it talks to, compare both directions of each contract: what this code produces against what the reference accepts, and what the reference produces against what this code accepts. Compare limits, field order, field types and versions. Read the reference in a **reference** related repo when one is listed, and cite it.
5. Specifications: when the change touches a wire format, a protocol message or a rule a specification defines, such as a validation or consensus rule, and a **reference** related repo holds that specification, search it for the name of the message, field or rule and compare the change with what it says. Example: a change to the \`PUBLISH\` packet of an MQTT client, compared with the section on that packet.
6. Error paths: a failure that is swallowed, loses its cause, or leaves state half changed.
7. Dropped cases: for each pattern match, \`switch\`, error handler, ignored result or fallback the change adds, name the variants and errors it leaves out and what happens to each. Examples: Rust \`let _ =\`, \`.ok()\` or \`unwrap_or_default()\`, TypeScript \`catch {}\`, \`?.\` with a default or an unawaited promise, Python \`except: pass\` or a \`dict.get\` default, Go \`_ =\` on an error or an ignored \`ok\`.
8. Same defect, other cases: once you find a defect, name the other kinds of input that reach the same lines and the other places in the change that make the same mistake. List every case that fails the same way in that one finding, each place as \`file:line\`. Example: a CSV parser that mishandles a comma inside a quoted field mishandles a line break inside one the same way.
9. Root cause: for a change that fixes a problem, ask: Does this fix the root cause of the problem, or is it a hacky workaround for the symptom? If it is a workaround, cite the code that causes the problem and say what change there would replace the PR's fix, or name the input or path that still fails. If you can't, say nothing. The same gap on a second path, or the change handling the same condition in more than one place, points to a root cause elsewhere: find the one decision that produces the condition and read that code before you write the finding. When the PR's own text calls something an intended workaround, that is a claim to check, never a reason to skip the question.
10. Tests: for each test the change adds or edits, name the changed line whose revert makes it fail. A test with no such line, one that sets private fields or internal state instead of calling the public API, or one whose mocks replace the code under test is a defect.
11. Reuse: for each constant, helper, type or loop the change adds, search for an existing one that does the same, and cite its \`file:line\`.
12. Repository rules: check each added line against the rules in the repository context, such as \`AGENTS.md\`, \`CLAUDE.md\` or \`CONTRIBUTING.md\`, and quote the rule it breaks.
13. Change rules: go through the instruction files under "Repository Context" for rules about the change as a whole: what a change must include, such as a test, a migration or a documentation update, and how it must be scoped. Report each one this PR breaks, on the changed line that makes the rule apply. Only a rule stated as a requirement counts ("must", "needs", "never", "always"), never advice ("prefer", "try to", "consider"). Example: a Django project's \`CONTRIBUTING.md\` says every model change needs a migration, and the PR adds a model field without one. Only the text under "Repository Context" counts. It comes from the base branch, so the PR's own edits to these files are not in force. An \`AGENTS.md\` or \`CLAUDE.md\` in a subdirectory, such as \`services/api/AGENTS.md\`, covers only changes under that directory, and \`.claude/CLAUDE.md\` covers the whole repository. Set \`"rule"\` to the rule quoted word for word and \`"ruleFile"\` to the file it comes from, as its heading names it. Report a rule that asks for focused changes only as the split rule under "Rules" allows.
14. Contract breaks: changed behavior of an API, config key, file or wire format, or stored data that existing code relies on.
15. Narrowed input: when the change narrows what it accepts, such as a lower or new limit, stricter validation, a narrower type, a new required field or a stricter format, find every producer of that input, in this repository or in a reference it mirrors, and check that each still fits. Examples: Rust \`MAX_FRAME_BYTES\` lowered below a frame a peer sends, TypeScript \`email\` made required in a schema older clients post to, Python \`max_length=255\` on a field an upstream API fills with longer text, Go \`MaxRecvMsgSize\` lowered below a reply an older client sends.
16. Readers: search the whole repository, other packages included, for each changed type, field, variant and function, and check that every reader still works, not only each caller.
17. Consumers: for each public type, function or serialized format the change alters, find its uses in each **consumer** related repo and check that they still work with the change. Start from the lines under "In the Consumer Repos" when that section is given. Example: a field renamed in a TypeScript SDK that a component of a React app still reads under its old name.
18. Sibling state: when the change checks, adds or removes an item in one collection, read the type that holds it and check everything else that holds the item or is computed from it: other collections, caches, indexes, derived values such as totals or counts, serializers and change notifications such as events or webhooks.
19. Sibling symmetry: compare each changed function with its counterpart, such as encode and decode, the other enum arms or the same function for another type. A check, version guard, lock or event that one has and the other lacks is a defect.

A PR can hold several defects of different kinds. Look for all of them, not only the first or the worst. Report a reuse finding only with the existing code's \`file:line\`, and a rule finding only with the rule quoted.",
}
